{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong upward-regime underprediction: when the last 10–15 days show a sustained uptrend (higher highs / higher lows), add a positive level shift to the whole path. In the examples, the model is typically ~+3 to +10 EUR too low, with the gap widening with horizon.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: apply an increasing uplift with forecast lead time (small at days 1–3, larger by days 10–30). The model’s slope is too shallow versus realized outcomes in rallies.",
    "Lag in trend acceleration: if recent returns are positive and accelerating (e.g., last 5–10 day momentum > prior 10–20 day momentum), steepen the forecast trajectory (increase daily drift) rather than only shifting the level.",
    "Over-mean-reversion after spikes: when spot has recently jumped (local spike), the model tends to pull forecasts back down too quickly; dampen mean-reversion strength and keep prices elevated for longer (delay reversion by ~1–2 weeks).",
    "Late-horizon downside overshoot: for horizons >20 days, the model sometimes projects a drop back toward low-70s even when the regime remains high; impose a regime floor/anchor near the recent 10-day average (or recent breakout level) to prevent unrealistic late-horizon declines."
  ]
}
